+# Hegel property-based testing report: `data-encoding`
+
+Property-based tests were added to the existing test file `lib/tests/lib.rs` (per the skill's
+"add tests to the appropriate existing test file" rule), using `hegeltest` 0.28.2 as a
+dev-dependency of the `data-encoding` package in `lib/`.
+
+The centrepiece is a `#[hegel::composite]` generator `specs(force_padding)` that builds
+arbitrary *valid* `Specification`s: all six bit-widths (base2/4/8/16/32/64), both bit-orders,
+`check_trailing_bits` on/off, optional padding (only where 8 % bit != 0, as the library
+requires), ignored characters, output wrapping (width a positive multiple of the decoded block
+length, < 256), and character translation (sources drawn from unassigned ASCII, targets from
+assigned characters: symbols, padding, or ignored characters). A second composite,
+`any_encoding()`, mixes these custom encodings with all 18 predefined constants. A third,
+`canonical_specs(bits)`, builds *canonical* encodings (unique ASCII symbols, either bit-order,
+nothing else) for the properties that only hold when `is_canonical()` is true.
+
+A second pass extended the original 16 properties with six validation/rejection-oriented ones
+(17-22 below): decode-then-encode canonicality driven by strings drawn directly from the
+symbol alphabet, constructed trailing-bits malleability spellings, corrupt-one-character and
+truncation rejection, encode-output padding shape, and an `encode_mut` differential.
+
+## Properties tested
