+
+The stateful test's model is the concatenation of all appended fragments; the subject is the
+`Encoder` with its internal 255-byte buffer and block-aligned flushing. Fragment sizes are
+drawn 0..=600 (size drawn separately, per the skill's large-collection guidance) so fragments
+larger than the internal buffer and the block length are exercised. The comparison happens
+after `hegel::stateful::run` returns (dropping the machine finalizes the encoder); a
+per-operation invariant on the output is impossible because the `Encoder` holds `&mut String`
+exclusively — an API constraint, not a test shortcut.
+
+## Test results
+
+All tests pass. Final `cargo test` in `lib/`:
+
+```
+test result: ok. 0 passed; 0 failed; ...   (unittests src/lib.rs)
+test result: ok. 55 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; ...   (tests/lib.rs, finished in ~1.9s)
+test result: ok. 42 passed; 0 failed; 1 ignored; ...   (doc-tests)
+```
+
+The 55 integration tests are the 33 pre-existing unit tests plus the 22 property tests above.
+Case counts were raised well above the default 100 for the higher-value properties since the
+suite is fast: roundtrip-custom-spec, decode-arbitrary, decode-mut-partial, canonical
+decode-encode roundtrip, and corrupt-one-char run 1000 cases each; most of the rest run 500;
+write-buffer runs 300. Total: ~11,500 generated cases per `cargo test` run, all passing.
+
+Because the whole property suite ran suspiciously fast (0.3s at default counts), I verified in
+a scratch project that hegel really executes the configured number of cases (it does — exactly
+100 per test by default) and that a deliberately failing property is caught and shrunk (it was:
+`assert!(n < 1000)` on an unbounded `u64` shrank to exactly `n = 1000`).
+
+The second pass additionally performed the skill's Run-and-Reflect checks on the six new
+properties, with temporary instrumentation (atomic case/branch counters, later removed):
+
+- **10x exploratory run** (every property's `test_cases` temporarily multiplied by 10, ~23s):
+  counters confirmed 10,000 cases actually ran for the instrumented tests. This run caught a
+  real over-assertion in `prop_corrupt_one_char_rejected` that 1,000 cases had missed (see
+  "Unsound properties" below) — shrunk to the minimal counterexample
+  `BASE32, data=[0], pos=2, junk=0`.
+- **Guarded branches fire**: `prop_decode_encode_canonical_roundtrip` took its Ok branch 7,292
+  times and its Trailing branch 2,708 times out of 10,000; `prop_corrupt_one_char_rejected`
+  reached the no-ignore branch 6,650 times and the exact-position (no-padding) branch 5,171
+  times out of 10,000. Nothing passes vacuously.
+- **Liveness**: all five other new properties were each broken by inverting an assertion
+  *inside* their guards; all five failed with cleanly shrunk counterexamples (e.g. the
+  trailing-bits test shrank to the base-8 spelling `[0,0,1]` of `data=[0]` — the same "001"
+  case as the crate's own `trailing_bits` unit test), then were reverted. The `.hegel/`
+  failure database created by these deliberate failures was deleted afterwards.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+None found. All 22 properties pass at elevated case counts (and at 10x those counts in the
+exploratory run) against both the 18 predefined encodings and arbitrary custom specifications
+covering every feature combination (bit-width, bit-order, trailing-bit checking, padding,
+ignored characters, wrapping, translation).
+
+One hypothesis investigated and dismissed: `encode_len` computes
+`olen + end.len() * div_ceil(olen, col)` for wrapping encodings, which looked like it could
+overflow (and panic in debug builds) within the documented domain `len <= usize::MAX / 512`.
+Working through the bounds shows it cannot: `olen <= 8 * len <= usize::MAX / 64`, the wrap
+width is at least the decoded block length (2, 4, or 8 depending on bit-width), and the
+separator is < 256 bytes, so the worst case is roughly `olen * 129 <= usize::MAX / 2` for
+base16 — within range for every bit-width. `prop_encode_len_documented_domain_no_panic`
+exercises this empirically up to the boundary.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **"Corrupting one character always yields a `Symbol` error" (weakened, second pass).** The
+  first version of `prop_corrupt_one_char_rejected` asserted that for encodings without
+  ignored characters, replacing one character with an Invalid byte produces `DecodeKind::Symbol`
+  at a position `<= pos`. The 10x exploratory run falsified it: for **padded** encodings the
+  decoder can diagnose the block as an invalid *padding length* before it ever looks at the
+  corrupted byte — `BASE32` `"AA\x00====="` (corrupt position 2 of `"AA======"`) reports
+  `Padding` at position 3, i.e. a position *after* the corruption. This is intentional library
+  behavior, not a bug: the crate's own `base32_error` unit test pins `Padding(3)` for
+  `"MAA====="`, the `Padding` kind's docs say "the index of the first padding character of the
+  invalid padding", and `decode_pad_mut` runs `check_pad` before the per-block symbol check.
+  The property now asserts `Symbol` at exactly `pos` for unpadded no-ignore encodings and
+  `Symbol`-or-`Padding` for padded ones (rejection itself is asserted unconditionally).
+- **"Canonical encodings reject everything that is not an encode output" tested via arbitrary
+  symbol strings.** The first pass abandoned a decode-then-encode test on the grounds that any
+  constructive generator for "decodable y" reduces it to test #2. That was too pessimistic: the
+  second pass implements the property soundly in two parts. Test #17 draws valid-length symbol
+  strings directly from the alphabet and asserts the accept/reject dichotomy (accepted implies
+  re-encodes to itself; rejected implies `Trailing` at the last position) — the branch counters
+  above show both sides are exercised heavily, so it does not degenerate into a rejection-rate
+  measurement. Test #18 *constructs* the non-canonical spellings exactly (all 2^t - 1 of them
+  for the drawn data) and checks them individually against both the strict encoding and its
+  permissive twin, which is precisely the malleability bug pattern the crate documents.
+- **Per-operation output invariant in the stateful Encoder test.** Not unsound but
+  unimplementable: `Encoder<'a>` exclusively borrows the output `String`, so no invariant can
+  read the output while the machine holds the encoder. The model comparison happens once,
+  after `run` finalizes the encoder (see above). If the `Encoder` API ever exposes a way to
+  observe output mid-stream, the test should be strengthened.
+
+No property failed and then got weakened; nothing was dropped because of a library failure.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+Overall the skill and Rust reference worked well — 15 of 16 tests compiled on the first try,
+including the parts I expected to fight (composite generators with parameters, a
+`#[hegel::state_machine]` on an `impl` block with an elided lifetime). Specific items:
+
+1. **MSRV conflict on `cargo add` (missing from Setup).** The reference's setup step
+   `cargo add --dev hegeltest` failed with:
+   `error: no version of crate 'hegeltest' can maintain data-encoding's rust-version of 1.48`
+   (`help: pass --ignore-rust-version to select hegeltest@0.28.2 which requires rustc 1.86`).
+   Old, widely-used crates are exactly the kind of target this skill gets pointed at, so the
+   Setup section should mention that a dev-dependency does not affect the library's MSRV for
+   downstream users and that `--ignore-rust-version` is the expected fix (or flag the tradeoff
+   if the project actually CI-tests its MSRV against dev-dependencies, as this one does via
+   `Cargo.lock.msrv`).
+
+2. **`.unique()` signature is wrong in the reference.** The reference documents
+   `generators::vecs(...).unique()` ("`.unique()` — All elements distinct") and Gotcha 9 shows
+   `.unique()` with no arguments. The actual API takes a bool:
+   `error[E0061]: this method takes 1 argument but 0 arguments were supplied` — the method is
+   `pub fn unique(mut self, unique: bool) -> Self`. `.unique(true)` compiles. Both places in
+   the reference need updating.
+
+3. **Package-vs-crate naming could use one sentence.** Setup says `cargo add --dev hegeltest`
+   but every code example uses `hegel::` paths. It works because the `hegeltest` package's
+   library target is named `hegel`, but nothing says so; I confirmed via `cargo metadata`
+   before writing 400 lines against the wrong assumption. Suggest adding: "the package is
+   `hegeltest`; the library it exposes is imported as `hegel`."
+
+4. **Stateful testing docs are good but undersell two things that mattered here.**
+   (a) Invariants are optional — a machine with only `#[rule]` methods compiles and runs; the
+   reference never says whether at least one `#[invariant]` is required.
+   (b) `hegel::stateful::run(machine, tc)` *consuming* the machine is a feature: for
+   builder/streaming APIs whose result is only observable on drop/finalize (like `Encoder`
+   here, which exclusively borrows its output `String`), you can have the machine hold
+   borrows, let `run` drop it, and assert the model comparison on the borrowed data
+   afterwards. Also worth stating that `#[hegel::state_machine]` works on impls with lifetime
+   parameters (`impl Machine<'_>`) — I assumed it might not and was pleasantly surprised.
+
+5. **No feedback that cases actually ran.** The full 16-test suite at default settings passes
+   in 0.3s with output indistinguishable from empty tests. I only trusted the green result
+   after building a canary project to (a) count executions with an `AtomicUsize` (exactly 100)
+   and (b) confirm a deliberately-false property fails and shrinks (it shrank `u64` to the
+   minimal counterexample 1000, and the failure output printed the draw as `let n = 1000;`,
+   which is excellent). A line in the reference like "to see that cases are being generated,
+   run once with `verbosity = Verbosity::Verbose`" would have saved that detour.
+
+6. **Gotcha 3 (".hegel/ in .gitignore") could say when the directory appears.** No `.hegel/`
+   directory was created by dozens of all-passing runs in this repo, which made me wonder if
+   the database was working; the canary project shows it is created (next to the package's
+   `Cargo.toml`) when a failure is recorded. One clause — "created on the first recorded
+   failure" — would remove the ambiguity.
+
+7. **Composite parameters of non-`Copy` types need a `.clone()` (second pass).** A
+   `#[hegel::composite]` taking `bits: Vec<usize>` fails with E0507 ("cannot move out of value,
+   a captured variable in an `Fn` closure") at the point the parameter is consumed
+   (`sampled_from(bits)`), because the generated generator closure is `Fn`, not `FnOnce`.
+   `sampled_from(bits.clone())` fixes it. Worth one line in the composite section of the Rust
+   reference, since passing a `Vec` of choices into a reusable generator is a natural pattern.
+
+8. **The 10x exploratory run earned its keep (second pass).** The over-strong corruption
+   property passed 1,000 cases and failed only in the 10x run — the counterexample needs the
+   corrupted position to fall in the padding region *and* the resulting pre-padding length to
+   be invalid, a boundary conjunction exactly as described in the skill's Generator Discipline
+   section. The temporary-case-counter advice was also right: the instrumented run proved the
+   extra cases actually executed rather than trusting wall-clock time.
+
+9. **What just worked, for the record:** `#[hegel::composite]` with extra parameters
+   (`specs(force_padding: bool)`) and composites calling other composites
+   (`any_encoding()` drawing from `specs(false)`); `one_of!` mixing `sampled_from` and
+   `integers::<u8>()` as a `vecs` element generator; `sampled_from` over a `Vec<Encoding>` of
+   the 18 predefined constants; `#[hegel::test(test_cases = N)]`; drawing sizes separately and
+   using `.min_size(n).max_size(n)` for size control. The skill's advice to mine fuzz targets
+   and doc "Properties" sections for evidence mapped one-to-one onto this crate's own fuzz
+   suite, which independently validated the property selection.
